@lorenzolfm If `Acquired<T>` is defined in `cdk-sql-common`, each mint storage crate could expose its own acquired type through an associated type and keep its construction inside that crate.

As long as the type implements `DerefMut<Target = T>`, it should work with the rest of the mint. That would address the main issue: the storage implementation would create acquired or locked values, rather than elsewhere in the codebase.

Something like:

```rust
/// Mint quote database writer trait
#[async_trait]
pub trait QuotesTransaction {
    /// Mint quote database error
    type Err: Into<Error> + From<Error>;

    /// A storage-specific mutable wrapper around a value
    type Locked<T>: DerefMut<Target = T>;

    async fn get_mint_quotes_by_ids(
        &mut self,
        quote_ids: &[QuoteId],
    ) -> Result<Vec<Option<Self::Locked<MintQuote>>>, Self::Err>;
}
```

This PR does a lil bit more than the issue asks, right? I think you're right to tackle the closely related issues too, but its worth discussing the approach a little more.

Let me give you an example:

This PR turns `version` into a field of `MintMetadata`

```rust
    /// Monotonically increasing snapshot version, used to decide whether a
    /// database still needs this snapshot written to it.
    ///
    /// Only a successful fetch from the mint produces a new version. Data read
    /// out of a database is not new, and bumping the version for it made the
    /// fetch that was running concurrently skip its write.
    version: usize,
```

But then on `load_from_mint` it also gets used to ask "did someone refresh the regular keysets while I waited for the lock?"

```rust
  let current_version = self.metadata.load().version;
  let _guard = self.fetch_lock.lock().await;
  // ...
  if current_metadata.status.is_populated && current_metadata.version > current_version {
```

The old `status.version` did both jobs too, but auth fetches didn't move it. Now they do, so a forced refresh can return old keysets without asking the mint. The same bump also lets auth-only fetches reach the persist.

The description says `version` moved so it isn't duplicated per data kind, but `is_populated` and `updated_at` are still per kind, and that `if` reads one of each. IMO a global `version` is the problem, and I don't think any of the fixes here need it. #2807 only needs the db read to stop bumping it.

wdyt?

Why do you want the wrapper implementing `DerefMut`?

Won't this allow `Acquired` to be updated to something that doesn't match what the dev locks?

  ```rust
  let mut quote = tx.get_mint_quote(&a).await?.ok_or(Error::UnknownQuote)?;
  quote.id = b;
  quote.add_payment(...)?;
  tx.update_mint_quote(&mut quote).await?; // writes b, we only locked a
  ```
